# Trivy exceptions.
#
# Every entry carries a reason. An undocumented ignore is indistinguishable
# from an oversight, and reviewing this file should be enough to understand
# which risks were accepted and why.

# AWS-0132 — "Bucket does not encrypt data with a customer managed key."
#
# Accepted, deliberately.
#
# The bucket holds avatar images that are served publicly by design: the
# objects contain no PII and no regulated data, and their keys are random
# UUIDs so they leak nothing about the users either. SSE-S3 (AES256) already
# provides encryption at rest and is enabled unconditionally.
#
# A customer managed key would add key policy management, rotation and a
# monthly cost without changing the risk profile of public images. The
# trade-off would flip immediately if this bucket ever stored anything beyond
# public avatars — revisit then.
#
# Note the bucket is NOT public: access is granted by policy only, public
# access is fully blocked, and avatars are intended to be served through
# CloudFront with an Origin Access Control.
AWS-0132
